# Coolify environment variables — TEST/STAGING template.
#
# Paste this into the Coolify resource's Environment Variables (use the
# "Developer view" to paste the whole block). Coolify deploys the base
# docker-compose.yaml only (no override, no host ports) and injects the
# SERVICE_FQDN_NGINX_4000 / SERVICE_URL_NGINX magic variables itself — do NOT
# set those here.
#
# The harvest config arrives via HARVEST_CONFIG_B64 below (the image bakes no
# config and repo-file bind mounts don't work under Coolify). Edit the YAML file
# in the repo, re-encode it, paste the new one-liner, and REDEPLOY the resource
# to apply — a container restart reuses the old environment.

# --- Database (internal to the compose stack) ---
# DB_NAME (cde), DB_USER (postgres) and DB_PORT (5432) default in
# docker-compose.yaml; DB_HOST / DB_HOST_EXTERNAL are pinned to the `db` service
# there. The password is the one value with no default.
DB_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME

# --- Redis (cache) ---
# REDIS_HOST is pinned to the compose service by docker-compose.yaml, so these
# only matter if you point this stack at a redis outside it. For a managed
# instance set REDIS_URL (it wins over host/port) or the individual settings
# below. The API (web-api/utils/redis.js) and the harvester's cache invalidator
# (cde_harvester/redisFunctions.py) read the same four — set them for both or
# the harvest will flush a different instance than the one the API is filling.
#REDIS_URL=
#REDIS_PORT=6379
#REDIS_PASSWORD=
#REDIS_TLS=false

# Cache warm-up, run by the harvester after a load that changed data: it
# replays the most-requested routes from the nginx access log so the site is
# warm before anyone arrives. Default base URL stays inside the compose
# network, so the warm-up never goes out through the public FQDN.
#CACHE_WARM_BASE_URL=http://nginx:4000
#CACHE_WARM_CONCURRENCY=8

# --- API / frontend ---
# Nothing to set. The SPA calls /api relative to whatever host serves it, so the
# Coolify FQDN is never baked into the bundle and a changed domain needs no
# rebuild. Anything that DOES need the public URL (emailed download links, the
# OpenAPI servers entry) reads Coolify's injected SERVICE_URL_NGINX — which is
# also why APP_URL is not set here.
# CORS_ORIGINS=*         # default
# ENABLE_API_DOCS=true   # default
# BASE_URL=/             # default

# --- Download scheduler ---
CREATE_PDF=False
# GMAIL_USER=
# GMAIL_PASSWORD=

# --- Observability ---
ENVIRONMENT=staging
SENTRY_DSN=
# Optional: enables sourcemap upload + release tagging in the frontend build.
# NOTE: the base docker-compose.yaml (what Coolify deploys) does not currently
# pass these as frontend build args the way docker-compose.production.yaml
# does — set them here for when that wiring is added, or if deploying via the
# production overlay instead.
SENTRY_RELEASE=
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=

# --- Prefect worker / harvest triggers ---
# No cron while testing — trigger harvests manually from the Prefect UI or the
# dashboard. Set e.g. "0 0 */3 * *" when ready for a schedule (applies on redeploy).
HARVESTER_CRON=
VERNACULARS_CRON=
# Leave false so a config-only redeploy doesn't kick off a harvest.
RUN_ON_DEPLOY=false
INCREMENTAL_MODE=true
# REGISTER_DEPLOYMENTS is pinned to "true" on the service by
# docker-compose.yaml, so a value here is ignored.

# --- Consumed by the separate harvest-dashboard service, not by this stack ---
# PREFECT_UI_URL=https://CHANGE-ME-prefect.example.coolify
# HARVEST_TRIGGER_ENABLED=false
# HARVEST_FLOW_NAME=Harvest Source

# --- Harvest configuration (base64, one line) ---
# This IS the harvest config: resolved first, before any mounted file (see
# harvester/cde_harvester/core/config.py:resolve_harvest_config_file).
#
# It is base64 rather than raw YAML because a multi-line env value does not
# survive the trip through Coolify's env editor -> generated .env -> compose
# dotenv parser: continuation lines get reindented and `#` comment lines get
# mangled. Base64 is [A-Za-z0-9+/=] on a single line, so there is nothing left
# to corrupt. A value that fails to decode aborts startup rather than silently
# falling back to a stale config.
#
# Generate/regenerate from whichever config file you want to deploy:
#
#     base64 < harvest_config.production.yaml | tr -d '\n'
#
# Inspect what a deployed value actually contains:
#
#     echo "$HARVEST_CONFIG_B64" | base64 -d
#
# The value below is the small two-server set for testing; encode
# harvest_config.production.yaml instead to go wider. On the next redeploy the
# worker re-registers one per-source deployment per erddap_urls entry.
#
# OBIS is omitted below for the test config. Uncomment obis_discovery in the
# decoded YAML to include it — the dataset list is resolved from the OBIS API
# at harvest time (OBIS Canada + OTN-OBIS + the Canadian EEZ), so nothing
# needs to be baked into the image. Re-encode with the command above after
# editing.
HARVEST_CONFIG_
